# sem-procedure — UX design sheet prompt
# One display mode (a status-annotated ordered procedure) shown at two
# widths: desktop example pane and narrow stacked mobile. Dark minimal-tech
# page hosting the light documentation panes, exactly as the reference
# site's example viewer stages them. Reference attachments are the judged
# renders from the snap harness (see reports/tag-sem-procedure.md).

schema: "0.4"
id: sem-procedure-ux-sheet
type: image
quality: high

prompt:
  text: |
    A precise UX design sheet for the SemText vocabulary element
    `sem-procedure` (a CSS-only, status-annotated ordered procedure —
    a runbook checklist with zero JavaScript), rendered as TWO example
    pages on a dark minimal-tech canvas (page background #0f172a) whose
    light documentation panes (card surface #f8fafc, hairline borders
    #e2e8f0) each hold one procedure. Status colours: green success
    #34d399, blue current #60a5fa, muted grey todo #94a3b8, red blocked
    #f87171.

    LEFT PAGE — DESKTOP procedure pane (four steps, full status
    vocabulary). Each step is a rounded card row with, at its left edge,
    a thin status rail and, in a separate narrow muted gutter column, a
    positional ordinal (1, 2, 3, 4 — CSS-counter rendered, never part of
    the step text). Show every lifecycle state as a distinct row:
    a DONE step (green rail, " ✓" after the text), a CURRENT step
    (blue rail, " →"), an UNANNOTATED step that simply carries no status
    attribute and therefore renders as TODO (grey DASHED rail, " ○"),
    an explicit TODO step written as status="todo" that looks identical
    to the unannotated one (dashed grey rail + ring glyph), and a
    BLOCKED step (red solid rail, muted body text, " ⛔"). Five rows in
    all, stacked with even vertical rhythm.

    RIGHT PAGE — the SAME component at narrow mobile width (390 px):
    rows stack full-width, rails and ordinals survive, step prose wraps
    naturally to two or three lines while the status rail and glyph stay
    pinned to their row. Same five rows, same five states.

    Typography: clean humanist sans for step prose, tabular ordinals in
    the gutter, monospace reserved for nothing here (no code inside the
    panes). Flat, engineered, restrained — a documentation specimen,
    not an illustration. Ordinals, rails and glyphs are quiet; the
    reader's eye lands on which steps are done, current, pending,
    blocked.
  negative: |
    no hidden, faded-to-invisibility or dropped todo steps (unstarted is
    visible, just grey), no hand-typed step numbers inside the prose, no
    ordinal rendered inline with the text, no visible kind badge or
    label ("runbook" is invisible metadata), no buttons, checkboxes,
    collapse arrows or any interactive-looking control (the element is
    static), no solid blocks occluding words, no decorative photos or
    glow, no watermarks, no browser chrome, no light-mode page canvas,
    no glyph orphaned on its own line away from its step text.
  style: flat dark technical documentation specimen with light content panes, minimal-tech design system

attachments:
  - path: /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/sem-procedure-fixture/sem-procedure-primary-render-1280.png
    role: reference
  - path: /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/sem-procedure-fixture/sem-procedure-primary-render-390.png
    role: reference
  - path: /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/sem-procedure-fixture/sem-procedure-class-form-render-1280.png
    role: reference

output:
  formats:
    - format: png
      filename: sem-procedure-ux-sheet.png
  dimensions:
    width: 1536
    height: 1024

requirements:
  pages:
    - name: desktop
      must_show:
        - ordinal numbers in a separate muted gutter column, one per step, never inside the prose
        - status rail per step: green solid (done), blue solid (current), grey DASHED (todo, both the explicit and the unannotated spelling), red solid (blocked)
        - status glyph after the step text: " ✓" done, " →" current, " ○" todo (both spellings), " ⛔" blocked with muted body
        - unannotated and explicit-todo rows look identical
      must_never:
        - never hide, ghost, or drop the todo rows — unstarted is visible, just muted
        - never render todo identical to done (dash pattern + glyph must differ)
        - never show a kind badge, authored numbers, or interactive controls
        - never occlude step text (occlusion belongs to sem-spoiler, not here)
    - name: narrow
      must_show:
        - same component, full-width stacked rows at mobile width
        - rail, ordinal gutter and glyph survive the narrow layout
        - wrapped prose keeps its status rail and glyph on the same row
      must_never:
        - never fall back to a different component shape at narrow width
        - never let the glyph wrap alone onto its own line detached from the text
        - never truncate step prose with ellipsis — wrap it

eval:
  pass_threshold: 0.7
  criteria:
    status_completeness:
      weight: 0.3
      description: All five rows visible and distinguishable; unannotated and explicit todo are identical (grey dashed rail + ring glyph) and fully legible.
      fail_signals: [todo absent or ghosted to illegibility, unannotated row missing the glyph, todo identical to done]
    ordinal_gutter:
      weight: 0.25
      description: Ordinals sit in their own muted gutter column on every row, desktop and narrow alike; none are typed into the prose.
      fail_signals: [inline step numbers in the text, duplicated numbering, missing ordinals on narrow page]
    parity:
      weight: 0.2
      description: Both pages read as the same component — rails, gutters and glyphs survive the width change unchanged.
      fail_signals: [narrow page shows a different component shape, dropped rails or gutters]
    restraint:
      weight: 0.25
      description: Flat documentation specimen; no ornament, glow, controls, badges or occluding fills.
      fail_signals: [decorative background art, buttons or collapse controls, kind badge rendered, text behind a solid block]
  reject_if:
    - any step prose is unreadable, truncated or occluded
    - a todo row is hidden, ghosted or rendered identical to done
    - an interactive control, kind badge or authored inline step number appears
    - the page canvas is light-mode or watermarks/browser chrome appear

tags: [semtext, vocabulary, procedure, runbook, steps, status, checklist, dark, specimen]
